WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:05.310
Both Harley Quinn and the Birds of Prey have
a not-that long yet pretty storied history

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.130
in the DC universe, and the Margot Robbie-led
flick pays tribute to both if you're paying

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:10.680
attention.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.660
Birds of Prey is all about Harley Quinn's
big breakup with Mr. J, but the Clown Prince

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.940
of Crime isn't Harley's only ex.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.550
As Birds of Prey's animated prologue explains,
Harley's heart was broken a number of times

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.160
before she hooked up with the Joker.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.740
However, Harley's one true love isn't a homicidal
clown.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.400
It's Poison Ivy, the botanist turned eco terrorist
who's proven to be quite a thorn in Batman's

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:32.920
side.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:37.900
In the comics, Harley and Ivy are a very popular
pairing, and Birds of Prey knows it.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.020
That's likely why the female face that turns
up on the prologue's cartoon slot machine

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.060
looks a heck of a lot like Pamela Isley.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.000
While it's not definitively Poison Ivy, it's
pretty obvious that Harley has a type.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.060
Birds of Prey isn't a direct sequel to Suicide
Squad, the movie that introduced Robbie's

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.180
Harley Quinn to the big screen, but it clearly
takes place in the same universe.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:02.760
While grocery shopping with Cassandra Cain,
Harley recaps her story, and summarizes the

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.320
events of her earlier film.

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.420
A few shots from Suicide Squad reappear in,
or were recreated for, Birds of Prey, when

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.740
Harley flashes back to her origin story.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.720
That connection opens the door to one of Birds
of Prey's best Easter eggs.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.940
While breaking into the Gotham City Police
Department in order to free Cassandra Cain,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.420
Harley catches sight of a "Wanted" poster
on the wall.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.020
You might recognize that man if you've seen
Suicide Squad.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:30.210
The man on the poster is none other than Jai
Courtney, who played Harley's teammate Captain

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.460
Boomerang in David Ayer's superhero flick.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.620
The DCEU may not be as interconnected as the
Marvel Cinematic Universe, but little links

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.310
like these are always nice, especially considering
that Boomerang is expected to come back in

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.620
James Gunn's upcoming Suicide Squad film.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.420
With a name like that, how could you expect
anything different?

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.980
"What the hell's wrong with you people?"

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.500
"We're bad guys, it's what we do."

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:02.160
Cassandra Cain plays a big role in Birds of
Prey, but she actually plays an even bigger

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.520
role in the comics as one of Batman's many
sidekicks.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:10.140
She's been Batgirl, a superhero codenamed
Orphan, an assassin, and one of the deadliest

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.800
women in the DC Universe.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.760
Cassandra Cain first appears in Batman #567
as the mute daughter of a deadly assassin

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.459
who raised her only to, quote, "speak the
language of violence."

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:26.090
That's a pretty comic book-y concept, which
is likely why the film makes some major changes

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:27.360
to the source material.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.329
In the Birds of Prey movie, Cassandra is a
hard-luck pickpocket with a rough family life

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:34.780
and a tendency to attract sympathetic older
female mentors.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.260
She ultimately ends up as an "apprentice"
to Harley Quinn, sporting a similar look and

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.240
an identical love of breakfast sandwiches.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.420
Considering that the comics have seen her
eternally passed over for a larger role in

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.680
the Bat-Family, maybe the DCEU's version of
Cassandra Cain made a better choice.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:54.580
After all, "Harley Quinn's kid apprentice"
is still a better codename than "Orphan."

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:57.540
"It's got a nice ring to it."

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.370
When Renee Montoya comes by Dinah's apartment
to ask her to be her woman on the inside of

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.100
Black Mask's organization, Dinah refuses.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.140
Black Canary tells Renee that she remembers
that her mom was a hero who was left to die

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.780
with no help from the police.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.320
While that's a pretty convincing reason for
anyone to stay out of the superhero spotlight,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:20.600
Dinah's superheroic mother is a reference
to one of the most confusing retcons in DC

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.100
history.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:26.100
Black Canary was one of the very first super-heroines,
and a central member of the Justice Society

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:26.860
of America.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.240
Eventually, the Justice Society of America,
along with Black Canary, were retconned to

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:34.400
exist on a separate Earth from the regular
DC Universe.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.540
Later, they were re-retconned into the main
universe, and the modern Dinah Lance was reimagined

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.880
as the daughter of the original.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.260
Birds of Prey wisely errs away from multiversal
hijinx in its runtime, but it seems that,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.100
no matter the universe, Dinah's mother was
always a hero.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:53.860
Helena Bertinelli's character arc in Birds
of Prey is arguably the simplest.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.420
When she was a young girl, her parents were
gunned down by a crew of mobsters led by Stefano

00:03:58.420 --> 00:03:59.160
Galante.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.879
That's a pretty dead-on adaptation of her
comic book origins one of them, at least.

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:08.370
In her most famous comics origin, Helena's
family is gunned down an assassin working

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:13.620
for a group of rival mobsters, most notably
Stefano Mandragora and Junior Galante.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:18.199
The movie combines these two mobsters into
one, presumably to keep the number of onscreen

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.520
assassinations within a slightly more manageable
scale.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:25.760
"Psychologically speaking, vengeance rarely
brings the catharsis we hope for."

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.480
"Yeah."

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.520
When she debuted in Suicide Squad, Margot
Robbie's Harley showed a lot of skin.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.520
Instead of the black and red long johns that
made her famous, Robbie's Harley sported hot

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.740
pants, a short, sparkling jacket, and a t-shirt
with "Daddy's lil monster" scrawled across

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.700
the front in faux -cursive script.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.360
Some fans loved the new look.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.480
Others found it sexist and needlessly objectifying.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.120
Even Robbie seemed mixed on it.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.580
She told The New York Times:

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.560
"I'm not wearing hot pants next time."

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:56.440
Still, that Suicide Squad look is iconic on
its own and Harley can't escape it entirely.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.539
In the animated prologue, Harley's wearing
her original Suicide Squad getup.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.160
Her old outfit pops up again later in the
movie, when Renee Montoya is looking for something

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.660
to wear.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.620
However, even when Harley is dressed entirely
differently, the Suicide Squad influence remains.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:14.760
If you look carefully, you'll notice that
Harley has a tattoo on her chest, which also

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.480
says "Daddy's lil monster," just like her
old shirt.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.360
Without Harley Quinn, we wouldn't have Birds
of Prey.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.560
Without the Joker, we wouldn't have Harley
Quinn.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.380
And without Jerry Robinson, we wouldn't have
any of them.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.520
While Bill Finger and Bob Kane get all the
credit for creating Batman, it was Robinson,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.260
an artist, who invented the Dark Knight's
archnemesis in 1940.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.440
Joker isn't in Birds of Prey, but his influence
looms large over the film.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.680
As such, it's only fitting that Birds of Prey
pays tribute to Robinson's legacy.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.819
When she's captured by Black Mask and his
goons and pleading for her life, Harley tries

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.140
to convince Roman that she's good at finding
things.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.240
As an example, she brings up a nude photo
of Eleanor Roosevelt that the Joker once had.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.750
When the scandalous picture went missing,
Harley managed to find it in a bird's nest

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:58.760
in "Robinson Park."

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.900
Played by Rosie Perez in Birds of Prey, Renee
Montoya is Gotham City's toughest and smartest

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.540
cop... even if she can't stop talking in '80s
cop movie cliches.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.920
Originally appearing in Batman: The Animated
Series before making the jump to comic books

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:19.110
in Batman #475, Renee's long been a fan-favorite
character and an important member of Batman's

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.080
supporting characters.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.919
Viewers don't get to spend a whole lot of
time getting to know Renee in Birds of Prey

00:06:23.919 --> 00:06:25.610
before the craziness starts.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:30.440
Still, there are some nods to Renee's most
integral qualities; most notably, her sexuality

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.380
and knack for superheroic mischief.

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:37.780
Ali Wong appears in the film as Ellen Yee,
Gotham City district attorney and an ex-girlfriend

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:38.640
of Montoya's.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:42.800
As far as we can tell, Ellen is an original
character for the film, but Renee's mixed

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.250
business and pleasure before she's dated Batwoman
in the comics, and former district attorney

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:50.380
Harvey Dent had a longstanding, one-sided
obsession with her.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.360
In Birds of Prey, Renee ends the film saying
goodbye to the GCPD and joining the titular

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.270
superhero team; although she doesn't take
a codename on screen, she's worked as a successor

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:04.460
to the Question in the comics, which seems
as good a codename for Montaya as any.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.659
In one of the most relatable moments in the
film, Harley tries to get over her breakup

00:07:08.659 --> 00:07:13.150
with the Joker by buying a wildly expensive
pet that she might not have the time, money,

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.460
or space to take care of.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:18.740
Slightly less relatable is the fact that she
chooses to buy a vicious hyena.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.520
Harley names the hyena Bruce, in a pretty
clear reference to Gotham City's most eligible

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.320
playboy billionaire, Bruce Wayne.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.880
However, what seems to be a spur of the moment
decision for Harley is actually a deep cut

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:31.900
reference to the character's long history.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:36.600
In the cartoons and comics, Harley's often
seen with her two pet hyenas, Bud and Lou.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:41.020
Even though Birds of Prey changes some details,
it's pretty clear that Harley without a hyena

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:43.320
pet is like Batman without his bats.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:47.040
She's also got Bernie, a taxidermied beaver
wearing a tutu.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:51.279
The entire movie is so wacky that it would
be easy to dismiss Bernie as just another

00:07:51.279 --> 00:07:55.570
piece of zany set dressing, but the stuffed
beaver is actually a popular recurring character

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.140
in Harley Quinn's own comics.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:01.360
As the story goes, Bernie originally belonged
to Harley's first crush.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:05.540
Young Harleen stole the taxidermied animal
when her boytoy was sent to juvie.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:10.199
Like in the movie, the Bernie of the comics
is dead, but since Harley's certifiably crazy,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:12.420
she treats him like he's alive and able to
speak anyway.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.620
Fans of the Injustice video game franchise,
which pits DC comic book characters like Black

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:21.569
Canary and Harley Quinn against each other
in Mortal Kombat-like fights, have found a

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:23.900
few similarities to the games in Birds of
Prey.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:28.300
During one scene in Birds of Prey, Harley
dresses up in a stylish coat and hat in order

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:31.720
to sneak into the GCPD, where Cassandra's
been locked up.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:36.200
Her outfit looks almost exactly like one that
Harley wears in Injustice: Year Two.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.180
There's even a near-identical shot in which
Harley tears her coat open, revealing heavy

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:41.340
armaments underneath.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.220
It isn't just Harley who gets the Injustice
love, though.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.640
Black Canary does, too.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.380
At the climax of the movie, Dinah Lance finally
unleashes her sonic scream, decimating the

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.840
Black Mask's goons.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.200
It's a big moment, and it should look familiar.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.960
Jurnee Smollett-Bell, who plays Dinah in Birds
of Prey, all but admitted on Twitter that

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.040
she based her pose on Injustice's version
of the Canary Cry.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:06.660
Birds of Prey doesn't have a full post-ending
sequence, but it does have a nutty little

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.540
treat for fans willing to stay in the theater
until the lights come up.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.680
As the final credits roll, Harley's voice
takes over the soundtrack.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.400
First, she makes fun of the audience for sticking
around even though the movie is over.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:20.420
Feeling some pity for her fans, however, Harley
decides to let everyone who's still in the

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:23.100
theater in on a little secret: "Batman f—."

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.700
And the audio cuts off before Harley finishes
spilling the beans.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:31.020
We don't know exactly what Harley was going
to say, but we do actually have a pretty good

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:31.520
guess.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:36.220
On the Harley Quinn cartoon on the DC Universe
streaming service, Harley made headlines for

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:40.660
suggesting that Batman has a very... intimate
relationship with the flying mammals he takes

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:41.700
his name from.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.180
"I always wondered what the batcave looked
like.

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.620
So this is the place where you f--- the bats!"

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:52.040
It sounds like Robbie's version of the character
was going to let moviegoers in on the slanderous

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:52.640
truth, too.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:53.360
Yikes.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.140
No wonder Warner Bros. cut the audio.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:00.180
"Well, a guy who dresses up like a bat's gotta
have issues."

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:04.820
Birds of Prey has a few musical interludes,
but there's only one that serves as a double

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:05.420
Easter egg.

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.780
While Harley is in Black Mask's captivity,
she briefly loses consciousness.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.620
When she does, she dreams that she's singing
"Diamonds Are a Girl's Best Friend."

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:18.020
Cinephiles will instantly recognize the scene
as a tribute to Marilyn Monroe's performance

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:19.370
in Gentlemen Prefer Blondes.

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:21.780
But there's more going on in that scene, too.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:26.280
Roman Sionis is also there, and surprisingly,
it's not the first time that Ewan McGregor

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:29.840
has been part of a musical number set to that
one specific song.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.500
Years before Ewan McGregor broke bad in Birds
of Prey, he played the lead in Baz Luhrmann's

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:37.940
Moulin Rouge!, which also featured "Diamonds
Are a Girl's Best Friend."

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:42.240
You might remember the scene when Satine enters
the film for the first time as part of a lavish

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.020
musical number, stealing McGregor's heart
forever.

